{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791201345,"nonce":"XLATUdfHFeAMl70qYxYUaxalIaY=","message":"Revised: Gltf reads the complete format as data, and GltfPose evaluates it the way glTF defines; the systems built on it stay the game's.\n\n**In ashui-canvaskit**\n- Gltf: node tree (GltfNode), meshes with each primitive's JOINTS_0/WEIGHTS_0 and morph targets (GltfPrimitive, GltfMorphTarget), skins (joints, inverse bind matrices), animations (GltfAnimation, GltfChannel, GltfSampler: STEP, LINEAR with shortest-arc slerp, CUBICSPLINE). Sparse accessors read.\n- GltfPose: play(clip, time) samples into node TRS and morph weights; update() works out world matrices into an f32 buffer, parents first; palette(skin, out) writes joint matrices ready to upload; draw(ctx) draws unskinned meshes where the pose puts them.\n- ash-simd under -D ash_simd: sampling as four-lane weighted sums, matrix products as splat-and-multiply columns; plain Haxe otherwise. Tests check both give the same numbers.\n\n**Not in the kit**: a player, clock, blending, IK, state machines, and drawing skinned or morphed vertices. Those are a game's, in its own ScenePass.\n\n**Measured** (Blinc's the_strangler: 315 channels, 141 nodes, 102 joints; one frame of sampling every channel, world products and the palette; M-series Mac, ash 2026-10-05):\n- AOT: 33us with ash-simd, 55us plain.\n- hybrid, 2000 frames: about 225-245us either way (the loop is not promoted within the run).\n- Buster drone read in about 0.9s hybrid (92 nodes, 39 meshes, 100 channels).","files":null}]}